92186049322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050830 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050830 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050830 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050830 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050830 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050830 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186051046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050830 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050830 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050830 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050830 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050830 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428380 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050830 #edb/ref 1031 "The Cartesian Equation of a Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050830 #edb/ref 1033 1807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050830 #edb/ref 1034 1.1105439411315527 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050830 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6a093d52-40f2-54b8-8982-db428e58bab5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119653 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4949" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119653 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(5,2,-2)$ and the plane $\\Pi$ with equation ![](images/f9/f9fe9fa5e18e286ac7e026df2667c7a8ce259cd2c0416efe85d66d2546358dfd.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119653 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "38fce106-2ba8-554c-ace3-a5731d15a635" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119653 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(4,8,2).$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, from the equation of the plane, we get a normal vector ![](images/9e/9e7b8bc888dbf7a764c0d5b9533758277b59e78d476a3cfe7bbfb2fcac7e7363.svg)\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $\\Pi$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-1)\\cdot 2+6\\cdot 4+4\\cdot (-4)|}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-2+24-16|}{\\sqrt{4+16+16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,6\\,|}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119654 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4950" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119654 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $Q(1,-2,-1)$ lies on the plane $\\Pi:\\mathbf{r} \\cdot \\langle 2, 0, -2 \\rangle = 4,$ find the shortest distance from $P(0 , -1 , 2)$ to $\\Pi.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119654 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "74693316-2e5a-5821-87a5-fdc5b8719c82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119654 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1,-2,-1⟩-⟨0,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of a plane, we get a normal vector $\\mathbf{n}= \\langle 2, 0, -2 \\rangle.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $\\Pi$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|1\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot (-2)|}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2+0+6|}{\\sqrt{4+0+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,8\\,|}{\\sqrt{8}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119655 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4951" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119655 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(3 , -10 , 3)$ and the plane $\\Pi$ with equation $2x+y-2z = -1$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119655 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1538dde4-ab62-502e-8efe-7f37628ab861" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119655 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=4$ and $y=-5$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y-2z & =-1 \\\\ 2(4)+(-5)+1 & =2z \\\\ 4 & =2z \\\\ z & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(4, -5, 2)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overrightarrow{PQ},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨4,-5,2⟩-⟨3,-10,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,5,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle a,b,c \\rangle.$ So, for the plane $\\Pi,$ we have $\\mathbf{n}= \\langle 2, 1, -2 \\rangle.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $\\Pi$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|1\\cdot 2+5\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-2)|}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2+5+2|}{\\sqrt{4+1+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,9\\,|}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119839 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119839 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119839 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119839 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119839 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119839 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592